      <!-- HERO -->
      <header class="project-hero">
        <p class="project-label">
          Mark VII Equipment Inc. • Summer 2026
        </p>

        <h1>Air Compressor Run-Time Limiter</h1>

        <p class="project-summary">
          Developed a retrofit device to monitor air-compressor run time and
          shut the compressor off when excessive operation is detected, helping
          prevent equipment damage caused by compressed-air leaks.
        </p>
      </header>


      <!-- LARGE PROJECT IMAGE -->
      <div class="project-hero-image">
        <img
          src="/projects/air-compressor/hero.jpeg"
          alt="Final Air Compressor Run-Time Limiter"
        />
      </div>


      <!-- PROJECT META -->
      <section class="project-meta">

        <div>
          <span>Role</span>
          <strong>Lead Engineer</strong>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>Focus</span>
          <strong>Industrial Controls</strong>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>Development</span>
          <strong>3 Design Iterations</strong>
        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- THE PROBLEM -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">The Problem</p>

        <h2>Preventing a leak from becoming a compressor failure.</h2>

        <p>
          When a compressed-air tank develops a leak, the compressor can run
          indefinitely while attempting to restore pressure. According to
          Mark VII service technicians, compressors were sometimes left running
          for hours or even days.
        </p>

        <p>
          Extended operation can result in overheating and eventual compressor
          failure, turning a relatively simple air leak into an expensive
          emergency repair. Existing car washes lacked an automated, proactive
          mechanism for detecting and stopping excessive compressor run time
          before this occurred.
        </p>
      </section>


      <!-- OBJECTIVE -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">Objective</p>

        <h2>
          Detect excessive run time and stop the system before damage occurs.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The objective was to develop a product that monitors an air
          compressor's run time and shuts it off when excessive operation is
          detected, reducing equipment damage, downtime for our customers' car
          washes, and service effort.
        </p>
      </section>


      <!-- REQUIREMENTS -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">Design Requirements</p>

        <h2>Designing around protection, compatibility, and usability.</h2>

        <div class="requirements-grid">

          <article>
            <h3>Detect</h3>
            <p>Identify excessive compressor run time.</p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <h3>Protect</h3>
            <p>Disable the compressor until the air leak is repaired.</p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <h3>Stop</h3>
            <p>
              Stop the car wash when the excessive run-time fault occurs.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <h3>Alert</h3>
            <p>
              Clearly notify the car-wash operator of the fault condition.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <h3>Retrofit</h3>
            <p>
              Install onto existing car washes without redesigning the system.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <h3>Compatibility</h3>
            <p>
              Work with the various air-compressor models used in the field.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <h3>Usability</h3>
            <p>
              Provide a user-friendly interface for car wash operators and
              service technicians.
            </p>
          </article>

        </div>
      </section>


      <!-- SYSTEM SOLUTION -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">System Solution</p>

        <h2>
          Current sensing, timing logic, and the existing control circuit.
        </h2>

        <p>
          Compressor operation is detected using a current switch. Timing logic
          monitors how long the compressor remains on and, after a configurable
          threshold is exceeded, opens a connection wired in series with the
          existing compressor-control circuitry to disable the compressor.
        </p>

        <p>
          A pressure switch can also signal a loss of air pressure to the
          car-wash controller, causing the wash itself to stop.
        </p>

        <div class="system-flow">
          <div>Air Compressor</div>
          <span>→</span>
          <div>Current Switch</div>
          <span>→</span>
          <div>Timing Logic</div>
          <span>→</span>
          <div>Control Circuit</div>
          <span>→</span>
          <div>Shutdown</div>
        </div>
      </section>


      <!-- DESIGN EVOLUTION -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">Design Evolution</p>

        <h2>
          Three iterations developed through testing, simplification, and
          service feedback.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The design progressed through three major versions as the system was
          tested, simplified, reduced in cost, and refined using feedback from
          service technicians and engineers.
        </p>


        <!-- VERSION 1 -->
        <article class="version-section">

          <div class="version-heading">
            <p class="version-number">Version 1 • Prototype</p>
            <h3>SmartRelay Prototype</h3>
          </div>

          <div class="version-images">

            <div class="version-image">
              <img
                src="/projects/air-compressor/v1-1.jpeg"
                alt="Version 1 SmartRelay prototype"
              />
            </div>

            <div class="version-image">
              <img
                src="/projects/air-compressor/v1-2.jpeg"
                alt="Version 1 SmartRelay prototype"
              />
            </div>

          </div>

          <div class="version-content">

            <div class="version-description">
              <p>
                The first prototype used a SmartRelay to track compressor run
                time, accept user inputs, control a warning buzzer, shut off the
                compressor, and provide operating instructions.
              </p>

              <ul>
                <li>SmartRelay control</li>
                <li>Current-switch detection</li>
                <li>Adjustable run-time threshold</li>
                <li>Warning buzzer</li>
                <li>Built-in user interface</li>
                <li>24 VDC power</li>
                <li>Baseline prototype cost</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="iteration-note">
              <strong>Why it changed</strong>

              <p>
                The SmartRelay provided more control capability than was
                necessary for the application, creating an opportunity to
                simplify the architecture and reduce component cost.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>

        </article>


        <!-- VERSION 2 -->
        <article class="version-section">

          <div class="version-heading">
            <p class="version-number">Version 2 • Simplified Design</p>
            <h3>Timer-Relay Redesign</h3>
          </div>

          <div class="version-images">

            <div class="version-image square-image">
              <img
                src="/projects/air-compressor/v2-1.jpeg"
                alt="Version 2 timer-relay prototype"
              />
            </div>

            <div class="version-image square-image">
              <img
                src="/projects/air-compressor/v2-2.jpeg"
                alt="Version 2 timer-relay prototype"
              />
            </div>

          </div>

          <div class="version-content">

            <div class="version-description">
              <p>
                Version 2 replaced the SmartRelay with a timer relay dedicated
                to monitoring compressor run time. Physical controls, indicator
                lights, and written instructions replaced the SmartRelay
                interface.
              </p>

              <ul>
                <li>Timer-relay control</li>
                <li>Physical button, switch, and LED interface</li>
                <li>Warning buzzer</li>
                <li>Current-switch detection</li>
                <li>Adjustable run-time threshold</li>
                <li>24 VDC power</li>
                <li>≈26% cost reduction from Version 1</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="iteration-note">
              <strong>Why it changed</strong>

              <p>
                The redesign retained the required protection features while
                simplifying the system and substantially reducing component
                cost.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>

        </article>


        <!-- VERSION 3 -->
        <article class="version-section final-version">

          <div class="version-heading">
            <p class="version-number">Version 3 • Final Design</p>
            <h3>Service-Informed Final Design</h3>
          </div>

          <div class="version-images">

            <div class="version-image square-image">
              <img
                class="v3-image"
                src="/projects/air-compressor/v3-1.jpeg"
                alt="Version 3 final Air Compressor Run-Time Limiter design"
              />
            </div>

            <div class="version-image square-image">
              <img
                class="v3-image"
                src="/projects/air-compressor/v3-2.jpeg"
                alt="Version 3 final Air Compressor Run-Time Limiter design"
              />
            </div>

          </div>

          <div class="version-content">

            <div class="version-description">
              <p>
                Version 3 retained the timer-relay architecture while
                incorporating recommendations from service technicians and
                adapting the product for easier integration into existing
                equipment.
              </p>

              <ul>
                <li>Timer-relay control</li>
                <li>Current-switch detection</li>
                <li>Adjustable run-time threshold</li>
                <li>Revised contact selection</li>
                <li>Revised LED and button colors</li>
                <li>Existing enclosure reused</li>
                <li>24 VAC power</li>
                <li>≈30% total cost reduction from Version 1</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="iteration-note">
              <strong>Final design progression</strong>

              <p>
                This version moved the project from a functioning prototype
                toward a practical product designed around field service,
                installation, usability, and cost.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>

        </article>

      </section>


      <!-- FINAL PANEL -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">Final Panel Layout</p>

        <h2>Packaging the Version 3 design into an electrical enclosure.</h2>

        <p>
          The final design consolidated the sensing, timing, operator controls,
          and shutdown hardware into an electrical enclosure intended for
          installation on existing equipment.
        </p>

        <p>
          Component placement and wiring were developed with installation,
          accessibility, and serviceability in mind.
        </p>

        <div class="large-image-placeholder">
          <img
            src="/projects/air-compressor/panel-layout.png"
            alt="Final Air Compressor Run-Time Limiter electrical panel layout"
          />
        </div>
      </section>


      <!-- ELECTRICAL DESIGN -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">Electrical Design</p>

        <h2>From system concept to electrical schematic.</h2>

        <p>
          The electrical design integrates current sensing, timing logic,
          operator controls, indication, and the shutdown interface with the
          existing compressor-control circuit.
        </p>

        <div class="schematic-image">
  <img
    src="/projects/air-compressor/electrical-schematic.png"
    alt="Final electrical schematic for the air compressor run-time limiter"
  />
</div>

        <div class="design-details">

          <article>
            <h3>Current Sensing</h3>

            <p>
              Detects when the air compressor is operating without requiring
              direct modification of the compressor itself.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <h3>Timing Logic</h3>

            <p>
              Measures continuous compressor run time and compares it against
              an adjustable threshold.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <h3>Shutdown Circuit</h3>

            <p>
              Opens the existing compressor-control circuit when excessive run
              time is detected.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <h3>Operator Interface</h3>

            <p>
              Provides controls and visual indication for operators and service
              technicians.
            </p>
          </article>

        </div>
      </section>


      <!-- TESTING -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">Testing & Validation</p>

        <h2>Refining the design through testing and iteration.</h2>

        <p>
          Each version was evaluated and refined as the project progressed.
          Changes were driven by functional testing, component cost, packaging
          considerations, installation requirements, and feedback from service
          technicians.
        </p>

        <p>
          Additional validation details can be added here as the project page
          is developed.
        </p>
      </section>


      <!-- IMPACT -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">Expected Impact</p>

        <h2>Preventing equipment failure before it becomes an emergency.</h2>

        <div class="impact-grid">

          <article>
            <span>01</span>

            <h3>Reduce Downtime</h3>

            <p>
              A leak can be identified and repaired before compressor failure
              takes the car wash out of operation.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>02</span>

            <h3>Reduce Service Work</h3>

            <p>
              Service technicians can address the underlying leak rather than
              replacing a compressor that failed after excessive operation.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>03</span>

            <h3>Avoid Replacement Cost</h3>

            <p>
              Compressor replacement was estimated at approximately
              $6,000–$7,000 for parts alone.
            </p>
          </article>

        </div>
      </section>


      <!-- WHAT I LEARNED -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <p class="section-label">What I Learned</p>

        <h2>Engineering beyond the initial design.</h2>

        <p>
          This project gave me experience developing an industrial electrical
          product through multiple iterations while balancing electrical
          functionality, component selection, cost, installation, usability,
          and feedback from the people who would ultimately service the system.
        </p>

        <div class="skills">
          <span>Industrial Electrical Engineering</span>
          <span>Product Development</span>
          <span>Electrical Panel Design</span>
          <span>Component Selection</span>
          <span>Industrial Controls</span>
          <span>User-Focused Design</span>
          <span>Testing & Validation</span>
        </div>
      </section>


